body:
Cloudflare’s Turn-Turnstile widget stopped a script that was trying to scrape a login form. The page returned a challenge that required a token generated by a real browser, and the usual workaround was to spin up a headless Chrome instance. A recent commit to the cfsolve repository shows a different path: a pure-Rust library that talks directly to Cloudflare’s endpoints and returns either a Turnstile token or a `cf_clearance` cookie, all without a browser process.

The repository ships a command-line tool and a library that can be embedded in any Rust service. By the end of this post you will know how to choose a browser profile, run the binary against a target page, and integrate the solver into your own code base.

## Pick a profile that mimics a real client  

cfsolve does not guess a user-agent string. Instead it impersonates a specific browser described by a JSON file in the `profiles/` directory. The repository includes `profiles/safari-26-ios.json`, which records the exact responses a Safari browser on an iPhone would send during Cloudflare’s browser-check phase. Each profile also points to a sibling directory that holds the decoded challenge payloads; the solver replays those payloads instead of executing JavaScript.

Only Safari and Chrome(ium) profiles are supported today. Creating a new profile requires capturing the decoded traffic from a real browser, either manually or with a proxy that records the payloads and stores them alongside the JSON file. Moving the payload directory breaks the solver, so keep the JSON file and its directory together.

## Install the toolchain and build the binary  

The project pins a specific Rust version in `rust-toolchain.toml`. In addition to the compiler you need CMake, a C/C++ compiler, libclang, and Git (the build patches BoringSSL). Once the environment is ready, the binary is built with the usual Cargo command:

```bash
cargo build --release --bin challenge
```

The resulting executable lives at `target/release/challenge`. No additional runtime dependencies are required because all network calls are performed by the library itself.

## Solve a Turnstile widget or clear a challenge page  

A minimal invocation that solves the first Turnstile widget on a page looks like this:

```bash
./target/release/challenge --page 'https://example.com/login' /tmp/solve \
    --solve --fingerprint profiles/safari-26-ios.json
```

* `--page`, URL to fetch.  
* `/tmp/solve`, an empty directory where cfsolve writes every request and response for debugging.  
* `--solve`, tells the binary to perform the solving step.  
* `--fingerprint`, path to the browser profile.

When the solve succeeds the tool prints a Turnstile token or a `cf_clearance=…` string to stdout and streams progress information to stderr. The log directory is useful if Cloudflare changes its challenge flow; you can replay the exact HTTP exchange that led to the token.

If the target page contains several widgets, select the desired one with `--sitekey`, `--action`, and `--cdata`:

```bash
./target/release/challenge --page 'https://example.com/login' /tmp/solve \
    --solve --fingerprint profiles/safari-26-ios.json \
    --sitekey 'SITEKEY' --action 'login' --cdata 'CUSTOM_DATA'
```

When you only have a sitekey and no page HTML, add `--blank-page` while keeping `--page` set to the real URL. cfsolve builds a minimal page internally and solves the widget.

## Embed the solver in a Rust service  

The library can be used directly from code, but it requires a few patches because cfsolve vendors low-level crates that Cargo would otherwise fetch from crates.io. Add the following sections to your top-level `Cargo.toml`:

```toml
[dependencies]
cfsolve = { path = "/path/to/cfsolve", default-features = false }
tokio   = { version = "1", features = ["macros", "rt-multi-thread"] }

[patch.crates-io]
btls-sys = { path = "/path/to/cfsolve/vendor/btls-sys" }
wreq     = { path = "/path/to/cfsolve/vendor/wreq" }
http2    = { path = "/path/to/cfsolve/vendor/http2" }
```

Setting `default-features = false` disables the command-line tools, leaving only the solver library.

A concise async example, taken from the repository’s README, demonstrates both widget solving and challenge clearing:

```rust
use cfsolve::{Browser, Options, PageSource, Turnstile};

#[tokio::main]
async fn main() -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
    // Load the Safari iOS profile
    let browser = Browser::read("/path/to/cfsolve/profiles/safari-26-ios.json")?;
    let options = Options::default();

    // Solve a Turnstile widget
    let turnstile = Turnstile {
        url: "https://example.com/login".parse()?,
        page: PageSource::Navigate,
        widget: None,
    };
    let token = browser.solve_turnstile(turnstile, &options).await?;
    println!("{token}");

    // Clear a challenge page
    let cleared = browser.clear(&"https://example.com/".parse()?, &options).await?;
    println!("cf_clearance={}", cleared.clearance);
    println!("{} cookies; protected page: {}", cleared.cookies.len(), cleared.page.url);

    Ok(())
}
```

The `Browser` instance is cheap to create once and can be shared across concurrent tasks. Each operation gets its own connections and cookie store, so parallel solves are safe. The only catch is that the `cf_clearance` cookie must be sent from the same IP address and user-agent string that the profile used; otherwise Cloudflare will reject it.

## The takeaway  

cfsolve replaces a full browser with a lightweight Rust library that replays recorded browser checks. Use the supplied Safari or Chrome profiles, run the binary with `--solve` and `--fingerprint`, or embed the library with a patched Cargo setup. The result is a valid Turnstile token or a `cf_clearance` cookie, obtained without the overhead of a headless browser.

*Source: [cfsolve GitHub repository](https://github.com/chris124567/cfsolve).*
